</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Inference-time scaling has emerged as a powerful technique for enhancing the reasoning performance of Large Language Models (LLMs). However, existing approaches often rely on heuristic strategies for parallel sampling, lacking a principled foundation.
To address this gap, we propose a probabilistic framework that formalizes the optimality of inference-time scaling under the assumption that parallel samples are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.), and where the Best-of-N selection strategy follows a probability distribution that can be estimated. Within this framework, we derive a theoretical lower bound on the required number of samples to achieve a target performance level, providing the first principled guidance for compute-efficient scaling.
Leveraging this insight, we develop <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>, a practical algorithm that dynamically determines the optimal number of sampled responses. <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> employs a language model-based predictor to estimate probabilistic prior parameters, enabling the decision of the minimal number of samples needed that satisfy predefined performance thresholds and confidence levels.
Extensive experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks (including MATH-500, GSM8K, AIME, and AMC) demonstrate that <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> significantly reduces sampling overhead while remaining better or on par with state-of-the-art reasoning performance. Our work offers both a theoretical foundation and a practical solution for principled inference-time scaling, addressing a critical gap in the efficient deployment of LLMs for complex reasoning.
The source code will be open upon acceptance at <a href="https://open_upon_acceptance" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://open_upon_acceptance</a>.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) have become a pivotal research area, given their ability to tackle complex cognitive tasks spanning mathematical reasoning, decision-making, and problem-solving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Recent advances demonstrate that inference-time scaling can substantially enhance reasoning performance by enabling the model to generate diverse candidate solutions, creating a richer space for comprehensive inference through answer aggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
This approach is particularly valuable for proprietary LLMs, as it can operate purely at inference time without requiring model modifications.
Although effective, these techniques face a fundamental efficiency challenge: the linear relationship between candidate solutions and computational costs leads to prohibitive token consumption.
This has created an urgent need for optimized scaling strategies that navigate the Pareto frontier between reasoning performance and computational efficiency, maximizing accuracy while minimizing redundant computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Parallel inference-time scaling, a dominant paradigm in this space, operates by generating <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate responses in parallel and selecting the optimal output via learned rules (e.g., verifiers) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> or consensus (e.g., majority voting) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
Current approaches fall into three categories:
verifier-based approaches that train auxiliary models to rerank candidates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>; data-driven methods that fine-tune LLMs on high-quality reasoning traces to improve solution quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>; and inference-time techniques that dynamically adjust the number of candidates per input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
While these methods demonstrate practical effectiveness, they suffer from significant theoretical limitations.
Current strategies rely heavily on empirical heuristics and implicit priors (such as learned verifier preferences or empirical token budgets) without proper mathematical formulation.
Crucially, the field lacks systematic derivation of these priors from first principles or rigorous quantification of the fundamental efficiency-accuracy trade-offs inherent in parallel scaling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents a foundational study that bridges this critical gap through three key contributions:
First, we formulate a probabilistic framework that formally establishes the optimality conditions for parallel inference-time scaling under i.i.d. assumptions, where the Best-of-N selection process follows an estimable probability distribution.
This theoretical formulation provides a principled understanding of scaling behavior in probabilistic terms.
Second, we derive a theoretical lower bound on the number of samples required to achieve any target performance level.
This result offers mathematically grounded guidance for compute-efficient scaling, representing a significant advance beyond current heuristic approaches.
Third, we develop <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>, an efficient algorithm that implements these theoretical insights in practice. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> dynamically determines the optimal sample size by employing an LM-based predictor to estimate prior parameters, ensuring minimal computational cost while satisfying predefined performance thresholds and confidence levels.
Our experiments demonstrate <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s computational efficiency: it automatically terminates sampling for simple questions while preventing excessive computation on intractable ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, this work presents the first comprehensive treatment of parallel inference-time scaling that: 1) establishes a rigorous probabilistic model of the scaling process, 2) derives fundamental limits on scaling efficiency, and 3) provides a practical algorithmic implementation that approaches these theoretical optima.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>LLM Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ability of LLMs to perform complex reasoning tasks has garnered significant attention and seen rapid advancements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Early successes with prompting strategies like Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> demonstrated that LLMs could produce step-by-step reasoning traces, significantly improving performance on tasks requiring logical deduction, mathematical problem-solving, and multi-step planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This foundational work has spurred the development of more sophisticated prompting techniques, including Self-Consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which generates multiple reasoning paths and selects the most consistent answer, and Tree-of-Thought (ToT) reasoning, which allows models to explore diverse reasoning trajectories in parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Parallel Inference-time Scaling</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advances, efficiently scaling LLMs at inference time remains a challenge. Parallel inference-time scaling has emerged as a key strategy to enhance LLM performance by enlarging computational resources during inference. This approach involves generating <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate solutions in parallel and selecting the best one using various methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Existing techniques can be categorized into the following three groups:</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verifier-based Scaling Methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve inference-time performance, many studies use auxiliary models, known as verifiers or Process Reward Models (PRMs), to assess the quality of candidate solutions generated by a primary LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. These verifiers predict the correctness of a solution or assign it a quality score, guiding the selection of the final answer by re-ranking the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> outputs. For example, some methods train verifiers using human feedback or generated labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Recent work examines how the interaction between generators, PRMs, and problem difficulty affects optimal test-time scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. However, relying on additional models increases computational overhead, and the strategies for determining <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> often lack a theoretical foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model-tuning Based Methods with High-quality Data.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some approaches aim to improve the output quality by enhancing the generator LLM itself. This is often done by fine-tuning the base LLM on datasets rich in high-quality reasoning traces or problem-solution pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Research efforts focus on creating specialized datasets for mathematical or coding reasoning to embed these capabilities directly into the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Techniques like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) or process supervision, where models are rewarded for correct reasoning steps instead of just final answers, also fall into this category <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. While these methods enhance the generator’s initial quality, they do not address computational efficiency during inference, which is a critical aspect of scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dynamic Inference-based Scaling Methods.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recognizing the limitations of fixed <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, several methods explore dynamic approaches to adjust computation at inference time. These techniques adapt the number of generated candidates or computational resources based on the input query or the ongoing generation process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. For example, some methods allocate more computation to complex problems or where initial candidate solutions show high uncertainty or disagreement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Approaches like dynamic decomposition break down solution paths into manageable steps, dedicating more resources to challenging sub-problems. The goal is to avoid unnecessary computation on simpler instances while providing sufficient resources for harder ones. While these dynamic methods improve efficiency, they often rely on heuristic rules or learned strategies without a clear theoretical framework for the trade-off between efficiency and accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These dynamic methods represent a step towards more efficient scaling. However, the rules governing the dynamic allocation are often heuristic or learned without an explicit, foundational theory of the efficiency-accuracy trade-off. Our work distinguishes itself by providing a first-principles probabilistic formulation of the parallel scaling process. This allows us to derive a provably optimal strategy for dynamically allocating compute to maximize accuracy per token and to quantify the theoretical upper bound for efficiency, addressing the lack of systematic, theoretically grounded priors in current dynamic scaling approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formulate a theoretical framework for inference-time scaling from a probabilistic optimality perspective (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and present <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>, an implementation that effectively achieves the goal of compute-efficient inference-time scaling (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Implementation of the Optimal Scaling ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preliminaries.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input question <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> processed by a large language model <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, which generates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate answers <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each answer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning steps <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i,t}\}_{t=1}^{T_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i,t}\}_{t=1}^{T_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A verification mechanism <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a Process Reward Model, PRM) assigns normalized scores <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{s_{i,t}\in[0,1]\}_{t=1}^{T_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{i,t}\in[0,1]\}_{t=1}^{T_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to each step.
Defining <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as an aggregation operator (typically averaging multi-step scores or using the final step score), we compute the overall verification score for answer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\mathcal{A}\big[\,\mathcal{R}(a_{i,t}\,|\,q,a_{i,{&lt;t}},\mathcal{M})\,\big]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\mathcal{A}\big[\,\mathcal{R}(a_{i,t}\,|\,q,a_{i,{&lt;t}},\mathcal{M})\,\big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The optimal answer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected through:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}=\argmax_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}}s_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}=\argmax_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Previous research on inference-time reasoning has primarily differed in their choices of the language model <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, verifier <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and aggregator <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, our work makes a fundamental shift in perspective by investigating the probabilistic distributions that govern these components.
It is a crucial distinction that sets our approach apart from prior studies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verifier Score Distribution.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us assume that the verifier scores <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed question <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> are samples for a random variable <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that is conform to a continuous probability distribution with probability density function (PDF) <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and cumulative distribution function (CDF) <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{S}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{S}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\sim f_{S}(s|\theta,q),\quad F_{S}(s)=\mathbb{P}(S&lt;s)=\int_{-\infty}^{s}f_{S}(x)dx," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\sim f_{S}(s|\theta,q),\quad F_{S}(s)=\mathbb{P}(S&lt;s)=\int_{-\infty}^{s}f_{S}(x)dx,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the parameters in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hyperparameters for generation (e.g., the temperature <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and Top-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> values).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distribution of the Maximum Verifier Score.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\max\{s_{1},s_{2},...,s_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\max\{s_{1},s_{2},...,s_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the maximum verification score among <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, which serves as the core selection criterion in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In Preliminaries. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
For any observed score <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal decision on whether to sample more candidates can be determined when we know the exceedance probability
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This represents the likelihood that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximal score in the complete population of possible verifications.
The probability can be derived by considering the joint event where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\leq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\leq s</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent Bernoulli trails, yeilding:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{P}(s_{i}\leq s)=[F_{S}(s)]^{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)=\prod_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{P}(s_{i}\leq s)=[F_{S}(s)]^{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which also defines the CDF of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Y}(s)=\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Y}(s)=\mathbb{P}(Y\leq s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The PDF of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Y}(s)=\frac{d}{ds}F_{Y}(s)=N[F_{S}(s)]^{N-1}f_{S}(s)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Y}(s)=\frac{d}{ds}F_{Y}(s)=N[F_{S}(s)]^{N-1}f_{S}(s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that both the PDF and the CDF of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are functions with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows a few examples of such PDF functions in different values of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Probabilistic Optimality.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sampling rounds, conventional methods (e.g., Best-of-N) confront a critical trade-off: whether to continue sampling (potentially finding higher-scoring answers) or terminate (to conserve computational resources).
Our solution derives the probabilistically optimal sample size
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as the minimal value satisfying:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y\geq s_{\min})\geq\alpha" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y\geq s_{\min})\geq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the quality threshold for valid solutions, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> represents the required confidence level.
From Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Distribution of the Maximum Verifier Score. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we reformulate the probability requirement as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-F_{Y}(s_{\min})\geq\alpha\implies[F_{S}(s_{\min})]^{N}\leq 1-\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-F_{Y}(s_{\min})\geq\alpha\implies[F_{S}(s_{\min})]^{N}\leq 1-\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Solving for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> yields the closed-form solution:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}\geq\left\lceil\frac{\log(1-\alpha)}{\log F_{S}(s_{\min})}\right\rceil," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}\geq\left\lceil\frac{\log(1-\alpha)}{\log F_{S}(s_{\min})}\right\rceil,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\cdot\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\cdot\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> ensures integer sample sizes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Implementation of the Optimal Scaling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Translating the theoretical framework (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) into practice raises a critical question: how can we reliably estimate the verifier score distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(s|\theta,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(s|\theta,q)</annotation></semantics></math>?
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> parameterize this distribution, where these parameters need not imply Gaussianity.
Direct estimation of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> fails, because at inference time, there are only a limited number of samples.
We propose a two-stage estimation method to address this challenge.
During training, we leverage abundant data to learn the underlying parameter trends <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.
At inference time, we refine these estimates using the available scored samples for each query <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving accurate distribution modeling despite limited observations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General Trend Modeling and Learning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable practical implementation, we incorporate error tolerance into our parameter estimation framework.
We model the parameters <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> as random variables drawn from their respective mean values <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> with Gaussian-distributed errors:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\sim\mathcal{N}(\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu}):f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu}),\,\sigma\sim\mathcal{N}(\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma}):f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\sim\mathcal{N}(\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu}):f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu}),\,\sigma\sim\mathcal{N}(\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma}):f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> govern the permissible estimation error bounds.
The Gaussian assumption is justified by the Central Limit Theorem (CLT), as these parameters represent aggregate statistics that converge to normality.
This formulation offers both theoretical stability and practical learning efficiency.
As the global trends <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> are determined by the language model, we implement this through fine-tuning with additional MLP layers:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma}=\text{MLP}(\text{LLM}(q|\theta^{*}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MLP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma}=\text{MLP}(\text{LLM}(q|\theta^{*}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the fine-tuned model parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Modeling the Verifier Score Distribution.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before refining the estimations, we need a hypothesis for the actual distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(s|\theta,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(s|\theta,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We propose a truncated normal distribution constrained to the interval <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
This choice reflects both the bounded nature of verification scores and prevents model overfitting.
The probability density function is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(s|\theta,q)\propto f_{S}(s|\mu,\sigma)=\frac{\phi(\frac{s-\mu}{\sigma})}{\sigma[\Phi(\frac{1-\mu}{\sigma})-\Phi(\frac{0-\mu}{\sigma})]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(s|\theta,q)\propto f_{S}(s|\mu,\sigma)=\frac{\phi(\frac{s-\mu}{\sigma})}{\sigma[\Phi(\frac{1-\mu}{\sigma})-\Phi(\frac{0-\mu}{\sigma})]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denote standard normal PDF and CDF respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MAP Estimation for Parameter Refinement.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set of observed scores <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{\|D\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{\|D\|}</annotation></semantics></math> for question <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we refine the initial estimates <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{\mu},\bar{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> through Maximum-a-Posteriori (MAP) estimation.
With the likelihood (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In General Trend Modeling and Learning. ‣ 3.2 Implementation of the Optimal Scaling ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)) and Gaussina priors of paraterms (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Modeling the Verifier Score Distribution. ‣ 3.2 Implementation of the Optimal Scaling ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)), the joint posterior when given a set of scores for sampled answers of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(\mu,\sigma|D)\propto f_{S}(D|\mu,\sigma)\cdot f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu})\cdot f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(\mu,\sigma|D)\propto f_{S}(D|\mu,\sigma)\cdot f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu})\cdot f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The MAP estimates are obtained by solving:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*},\sigma^{*}=\argmax_{\mu,\sigma}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{\|D\|}\log f_{S}(s_{k}|{\mu},{\sigma})+\log f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu})+\log f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma})\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*},\sigma^{*}=\argmax_{\mu,\sigma}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{\|D\|}\log f_{S}(s_{k}|{\mu},{\sigma})+\log f_{\mu}(\mu|\bar{\mu},\sigma_{\mu})+\log f_{\sigma}(\sigma|\bar{\sigma},\sigma_{\sigma})\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adaptive Scaling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the estimated <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In Probabilistic Optimality. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Framework: Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we derive a question-specific and closed-form optimal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to determine when to stop.
This adaptive strategy optimizes computational efficiency: it terminates early for simple questions to avoid unnecessary computation and limits prolonged processing for intractable ones.
We name this approach <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> on the following representative math reasoning benchmarks:
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MATH-500</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>: A challenging subset of the MATH dataset with 500 high school competition problems across algebra, geometry, number theory, and calculus, following the OpenAI evaluation split.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>: A dataset of 8.5K grade school word problems assessing basic arithmetic and textual reasoning.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME 2024</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME 2025<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/math-ai/aime25" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/math-ai/aime25</a><span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">.</span></span></span></span></span>: Each contains 30 pre-Olympiad level problems from the American Invitational Mathematics Examination, designed to test advanced mathematical reasoning. <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMC<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">2</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/math-ai/amc23" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/math-ai/amc23</a><span id="footnote2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">.</span></span></span></span></span>: A collection of intermediate-level problems from the 2023 American Mathematics Competitions, bridging standard high school and AIME difficulty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Backbone Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess generalizability and efficiency, we use two open-source backbone LLMs of different scales and architectures: 1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>: A 1B-parameter instructional model from Meta’s Llama 3.2 series, ideal for evaluating inference-time scaling techniques with a relatively small model size, even increasing compute. 2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>: A 7B-parameter reasoning model distilled from larger DeepSeek R1 models into a Qwen model, representing a state-of-the-art distilled model optimized for complex reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline Methods.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> against a comprehensive set of inference-time scaling methods, including:
1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best-of-N (BoN)</span>: It samples <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning paths, with a Process Reward Model (PRM) scoring on them and then selects the best answer.
2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Consistency (SC)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>: It samples multiple diverse responses and aggregates the final answer based on majority voting.
3) Multi-round Thinking <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MR-Thinking</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>: It iteratively refines the reasoning process through multiple rounds.
4) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Beam Search</span>: It explores the most probable sequence of tokens during decoding by maintaining a fixed number of candidate sequences.
5) Diverse Verifier Tree Search (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DVTS</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: It is an extension of beam search that splits the initial beams into independent subtrees, which are then expanded greedily using a PRM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>, we employ separate predictors for the mean and standard deviation of the data point distribution, conditioned on the question context and initial samples. These predictors are trained for 30 epochs with a learning rate of 1e-5, weight decay of 0.01, and gradient clipping at 0.1. The linear layer includes a dropout rate of 0.2.
For scoring, we use the Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B as the process reward model (PRM). During maximum likelihood estimation (MLE), we vary the number of sampled data points within 3 to 10, which is dynamically determined according to the maximum number of generations.
To ensure fair comparison, we re-ran all baseline methods under the same experimental conditions. For all datasets, we use a sampling temperature of 0.7 and a top-p of 0.95. All experiments are conducted in one NVIDIA A6000 server with 8 GPUs. We report accuracy and completion token usage as the evaluation results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Can <span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> Ensure Optimal Scaling?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate scaling efficiency, we conducted comprehensive experiments across the four benchmark datasets, varying the maximum number of answer candidates from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math>.
This experimental design required 240 total runs per method (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> datasets <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math> candidate limits), representing a significant computational investment.
Due to these resource constraints, we focused our comparison on the two most representative baseline methods: Best-of-N (BoN) and Self-Consistency (SC).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that our <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> robustly achieves optimal scaling. We conduct a grid search over different threshold scores, ranging from 0.95 to 0.98, and confidence probabilities, from 0.01 to 0.1.
The results demonstrate that <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s performance closely follows the Pareto frontier (red dashed line), achieving near-optimal efficiency.
This confirms its ability to maintain scaling optimality across configurations.
Notably, all <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> implementations along the frontier consistently outperform baselines, delivering higher accuracy with lower computational cost (token consumption).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (II) demonstrates that <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> is optimal across various datasets in terms of performance-to-computation tradeoffs. For each method, we plot accuracy against the average token count consumed. The results reveal several key insights:
1) <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> consistently outperforms both baseline methods across all datasets, achieving either higher accuracy with comparable token consumption or similar accuracy with substantially fewer tokens. On GSM8K with LLaMA1B, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> reaches 83% accuracy with approximately 8,000 tokens, while Best-of-N requires nearly 12,000 tokens to achieve the same performance – a 33% reduction in computational cost.
2) The performance gap is particularly apparent on more challenging datasets. On AIME24 with Qwen7B, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> achieves 50% accuracy while Best-of-N plateaus at 47%, demonstrating that our method’s adaptive allocation strategy is especially beneficial for complex reasoning tasks where problem difficulty varies significantly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22376v1/performance.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scaling efficiency comparisons (accuracy vs. average token consumption):
(I) <span id="S4.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> versus the Pareto frontier and Best-of-N, demonstrating near-optimal performance;
(II) <span id="S4.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> against Best-of-N and Self-Consistency on four benchmarks, showing consistently faster convergence.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22376v1/appendix_performance.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/148;" width="476" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scaling efficiency comparisons (accuracy vs. average token consumption), with Pareto frontiers highlighted.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows more of <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s optimal performance across a broader range of datasets and models.
Besides consistently consuming significantly fewer tokens than BoN at the same accuracy levels, more remarkably, through proper dynamic allocation of inference-time compute, <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> sometimes achieves accuracy levels that BoN fails to reach even when scaled indefinitely to a substantial amount of tokens, as Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (b) demonstrates. These advantages stem primarily from <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s ability to evaluate question difficulty in advance and allocate resources optimally through our probabilistic framework. Notably, our case study reveals that excessive scaling can actually degrade performance by generating too many incorrect answers, which OptScale avoids. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix: Case Studies ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> presents some real cases as illustrative examples to show this phenomenon.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22376v1/distributions.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/198;" width="476" height="198"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Verifier score distribution: Real vs. estimated: <span id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> accurately estimates most distributions but shows reduced accuracy for multi-modal distributions with significantly varying peaks (Questions 5 and 10).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Can <span id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> Estimate the Verifier Score Distribution Accurately?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our approach, we compare the estimated distributions against the ground truth.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2 Can OptScale Ensure Optimal Scaling? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows various alignment cases between our modeled distributions and empirical observations.
In most cases (e.g., Questions 1, 2, 3, and 7), the ground truth verifier scores follow a unimodal distribution, which is accurately captured by a truncated normal distribution over [0,1].
For bimodal distributions (e.g., Questions 4, 6, 8, 9), <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> maintains reasonably accurate predictions.
Only in rare instances do we encounter more complex distributions: cases with multiple peaks (Question 10) or extremely narrow, high peaks (Question 5) challenge the truncated normal approximation.
However, these exceptions are statistically insignificant and do not materially impact overall prediction quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>How <span id="S4.SS4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> is Sensitive to Hyperparameters?</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22376v1/ablation_results_by_target_score.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/168;" width="471" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Sensitivity analysis of quality threshold <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>: Model performance across target levels.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22376v1/ablation_results_by_confidence.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/168;" width="471" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Sensitivity analysis of confidence level <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>: Model performance across target levels.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> employs two hyperparameters, namely the quality threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> and the desired confidence level <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
We repeat 66 combinations of these two parameters by varying <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> in the range of [0.80, 0.99] and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in [0.90, 0.99] with a step size of 0.02 and 0.01 respectively to evaluate how sensitive <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s performance on accuracy and completion tokens is with regard to hyperparameters. We employ Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct backbone with GSM8K benchmark for this focused study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of the Quality Threshold.</span> Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 How OptScale is Sensitive to Hyperparameters? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the changes in accuracy, token consumption, and efficiency (accuracy per token consumption) corresponding to the quality threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> changes. As <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the accuracy growth gradually slows down and converges. However, the required token consumption continues to grow exponentially, with the token consumption multiplying by 2.5 times when the quality threshold increases from 0.8 to 0.99. This trend results in a persistent decline in efficiency from 0.00016 to 0.0006, with the rate of decrease becoming increasingly steep.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of the Confidence Level <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Similarly, Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4 How OptScale is Sensitive to Hyperparameters? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the metrics changes along different confidence level <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Both accuracy and the number of completion tokens grow with <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. However, different from the exponentially growing pattern elaborated above <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of completion tokens grows almost linearly, increasing 100 tokens per 0.01 increase in confidence. The overall efficiency only drops slightly as the confidence grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we find that the quality threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> is a much more sensitive hyperparameter in terms of determining both accuracy and token consumption compared to the confidence level <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, making the selection of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> much more important than <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. During empirical experiments, we noticed that choosing a very high quality threshold (e.g., 0.99) could harm efficiency and result in suboptimal utilization of completion tokens, while a low quality threshold of below 0.9 could lead to accuracy degradation. A quality threshold <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> of around 0.95 to 0.96 is usually recommended, while there’s much more space in selecting confidence level <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>: the default could be set to 0.9.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Can <span id="S4.SS5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> Achieve a New SOTA Performance across Benchmarks?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the overall performance of our method with all baseline methods in both accuracy and token consumption.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.5 Can OptScale Achieve a New SOTA Performance across Benchmarks? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) accuracy superiority in 9 out of 10 experimental
configurations, with an average improvement of 50.40% over competing methods. Moreover, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> maintains this advantage while consuming substantially fewer tokens than comparable methods. For instance, on MATH-500 with DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> attains 93.2% accuracy while reducing token usage by 67.52% versus BoN. On GSM8K with LLaMA-3.2-1B-Instruct, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> reduces token consumption by 38.67%, demonstrating its strong ability to balance optimal accuracy performance and optimal efficiency.
These results highlight <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span>’s advantage in delivering robust and efficient reasoning performance across diverse reasoning benchmarks.
Compared to sequential scaling methods such as MR-Thinking, which often achieve lower token consumption when using reasoning models like DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, the token consumption gap here is largely due to the inherent limitations of sampling-based strategies in parallel scaling. In contrast, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> balances parallel efficiency with robust accuracy gains.
As detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Implementation of the Optimal Scaling ‣ 3 Inference-time Scaling with Probabilistic Optimality ‣ Probabilistic Optimality for Inference-time Scaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, a key advantage of <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> is its compatibility with a wide range of existing methods. Since it operates in a distinct scaling dimension, orthogonal to the reasoning or decoding strategies, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> can be seamlessly integrated with other techniques such as search-based inference or dynamic verification.
Rather than competing with these methods, <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OptScale</span> serves as a general-purpose optimization layer that enhances both performance and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of different inference-time scaling methods on common mathematical reasoning benchmarks (fixed <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=60</annotation></semantics></math>). “Acc.” denotes accuracy (%), “Toks.” indicate the total number of inference tokens.</span></figcaption>
